**New Evidence**
The chapter is dominated by 2022 delivery and program-implementation material, so I kept the new set mostly to primary ministry releases plus one WHO source for independent corroboration.

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Reaching life-saving vaccines to all","publisher":"WHO India","publication_date":"2022-03-15","url":"https://www.who.int/india/news-room/feature-stories/detail/reaching-life-saving-vaccines-to-all","claim":"India launched Intensified Mission Indradhanush 4.0 on 7 February 2022 to enhance Universal Immunization Programme coverage across 416 districts in 33 states and union territories after a pandemic-era fall in DPT3 coverage.","locator":"opening paragraphs","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent WHO feature story with dated 2022 policy and rollout details; strong corroboration for government immunization action.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 target-period public-health action.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to the Modi government’s health policy, though implementation depended on states and frontline workers.","contrary_evidence":["The source emphasizes coverage catch-up after a pandemic backslide, not a completed outcome.","It documents a launch and rollout plan more than final measured gains."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.5","6B.6"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"India crosses a significant milestone in the journey towards Universal Health Coverage - 1,50,000 Ayushman Bharat Health and Wellness Centres operationalized","publisher":"Press Information Bureau, Government of India / Ministry of Health and Family Welfare","publication_date":"2022-12-30","url":"https://www.pib.gov.in/PressReleasePage.aspx?PRID=1887497&lang=2&reg=48","claim":"India surpassed 150,000 operational Ayushman Bharat Health and Wellness Centres by 31 December 2022, with more than 86.90 crore screenings, over 8.5 crore teleconsultations, and 1.60 crore wellness sessions conducted through the centres.","locator":"headline and opening paragraph","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary government implementation report with specific, dated delivery metrics.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 target-period health-service delivery evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to the Modi government’s health policy; the rollout was shared with states and health workers.","contrary_evidence":["This is a government self-report and may overstate success relative to independent outcome measures.","Counts of screenings and teleconsultations do not by themselves prove equity or quality."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.4","6B.5","6B.6","6B.9","6B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Year End Review –Ministry of Education","publisher":"Press Information Bureau, Government of India / Ministry of Education","publication_date":"2022-12-30","url":"https://www.pib.gov.in/PressReleasePage.aspx?PRID=1887647&lang=2&reg=48","claim":"Samagra Shiksha was aligned with NEP 2020, PM-SHRI was approved to develop 14,500 schools, ICT labs were approved in 1,20,614 schools and smart classrooms in 82,120 schools, and Vidyanjali reached 10.85 lakh students in 2022.","locator":"headline and Samagra Shiksha section","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary ministry year-end review with concrete school-system inputs and coverage figures.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 target-period education-policy and implementation evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to the Modi government through central education policy, with delivery shared across states and schools.","contrary_evidence":["The source is a ministry self-assessment rather than an external evaluation.","Inputs such as infrastructure and volunteer reach do not directly prove learning outcomes."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.4","6B.5","6B.9","6B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Year-End Review-2022: Ministry of Rural Development","publisher":"Press Information Bureau, Government of India / Ministry of Rural Development","publication_date":"2022-12-30","url":"https://www.pib.gov.in/PressReleaseIframePage.aspx?PRID=1887438&lang=2&reg=48","claim":"MGNREGA provided employment to 11.37 crore households and PMAY-G had 2.11 crore houses completed by 15 December 2022, with the ministry also reporting broad DAY-NRLM coverage.","locator":"Key Initiatives and Achievements section","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary ministry year-end review with clear employment and housing counts.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 target-period rural welfare evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to the Modi government’s rural-development apparatus, though implementation is shared with states and local institutions.","contrary_evidence":["MGNREGA employment also reflects rural distress, not just welfare success.","Completion counts do not by themselves establish quality or durability of housing."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.6","6B.8","6B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Year End Review-2022: Ministry of Women and Child Development","publisher":"Press Information Bureau, Government of India / Ministry of Women and Child Development","publication_date":"2022-12-21","url":"https://www.pib.gov.in/Pressreleaseshare.aspx?PRID=1885352&lang=2&reg=48","claim":"The ministry reorganized its welfare work into Saksham Anganwadi & Poshan 2.0, Mission Shakti, and Mission Vatsalya; 40,000 Saksham Anganwadis were identified in FY 2022-23 and more than 15 crore Poshan Maah activities were conducted.","locator":"opening summary and Mission Saksham Anganwadi and Poshan 2.0 section","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary year-end ministry review with concrete scheme redesign and implementation counts.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 target-period child and women welfare policy evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to the Modi government’s social-policy choices, though field delivery depended on states and local workers.","contrary_evidence":["The source is a ministry self-report.","Scheme restructuring and event counts do not by themselves prove improved nutrition or protection outcomes."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.4","6B.5","6B.8","6B.9","6B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Year End Review 2022: Ministry of Labour and Employment","publisher":"Press Information Bureau, Government of India / Ministry of Labour & Employment","publication_date":"2022-12-29","url":"https://www.pib.gov.in/PressReleasePage.aspx?PRID=1887384&lang=2&reg=48","claim":"eShram had more than 28.5 crore registered workers, ESIC launched an online maternity-claim portal for 58.69 lakh insured women, and the ministry reported 2,297 job fairs in 2022.","locator":"headline and opening section","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary ministry year-end review with large-scale labor-protection and service-delivery figures.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 target-period labor-protection evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to the Modi government’s labor policy and digital social-protection infrastructure.","contrary_evidence":["Registry counts and job fairs do not by themselves prove job quality or income gains.","The source is a ministry self-report rather than independent evaluation."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.4","6B.7","6B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Year-End Review of Department of Food and Public Distribution– 2022","publisher":"Press Information Bureau, Government of India / Ministry of Consumer Affairs, Food & Public Distribution","publication_date":"2022-12-12","url":"https://www.pib.gov.in/Pressreleaseshare.aspx?PRID=1882759&lang=2&reg=3","claim":"PMGKAY continued through Phase VII in 2022 with about 1,118 LMT foodgrains allocated in total, and ONORC exceeded 93 crore portability transactions delivering more than 177 LMT of foodgrains.","locator":"PMGKAY and One Nation One Ration Card sections","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary ministry review with specific program totals and beneficiary-access metrics.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 target-period food-security and portability evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to the Modi government’s food-security policy, with fair-price-shop and state execution shared across levels of government.","contrary_evidence":["This is emergency and food-security relief, not proof of long-run food-system resilience.","Administrative distribution totals do not directly show household nutrition quality."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.6","6B.7","6B.8","6B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Year End Review 2022: Department of Drinking Water and Sanitation, Ministry of Jal Shakti","publisher":"Press Information Bureau, Government of India / Ministry of Jal Shakti","publication_date":"2022-12-27","url":"https://www.pib.gov.in/Pressreleaseshare.aspx?PRID=1886953&lang=2&reg=48","claim":"By 21 December 2022 more than 10.76 crore rural households had tap-water connections, and 84.83% of schools and 80.79% of anganwadi centres had potable tap water under Jal Jeevan Mission.","locator":"headline and sections on household coverage and public institutions","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary government year-end review with concrete household and institution coverage figures.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 target-period water-access evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to the Modi government’s water-infrastructure program, though delivery was shared with states and local bodies.","contrary_evidence":["Coverage figures are administrative and do not fully capture water quality or reliability.","The program was still incomplete in rural India by the end of 2022."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.6","6B.8","6B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Year End Review 2022: Ministry of Tribal Affairs","publisher":"Press Information Bureau, Government of India / Ministry of Tribal Affairs","publication_date":"2022-12-31","url":"https://www.pib.gov.in/PressReleaseIframePage.aspx?PRID=1887716&lang=2&reg=48","claim":"The ministry reported that 26.37 lakh tribal students received scholarships through DBT from 1 April to 31 December 2022, and the Cabinet approved Rs 28,920 crore for 452 new Eklavya Model Residential Schools, upgrades to 211 existing schools, and 15 centres of excellence for sports.","locator":"headline and major highlights section","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary ministry year-end review with clear distribution and institution-building figures for a vulnerable group.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 target-period vulnerable-group evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to the Modi government’s tribal policy, with some delivery through state and school systems.","contrary_evidence":["Scholarship counts do not prove completion or learning outcomes.","The EMRS approval is a long-term investment, not an immediate welfare result."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.4","6B.8","6B.9","6B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Year End Review- 2022 of the Department of Empowerment of Persons with Disabilities","publisher":"Press Information Bureau, Government of India / Ministry of Social Justice & Empowerment","publication_date":"2022-12-24","url":"https://www.pib.gov.in/Pressreleaseshare.aspx?PRID=1886365&lang=2&reg=48","claim":"The department said it sought to build an inclusive society for persons with disabilities and in 2022 held sign-language events at 3,200 places, signed MoUs with Amazon and Flipkart for skills and employment pathways, and expanded rehabilitation services through new and upgraded centres.","locator":"opening summary and major achievements sections","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary ministry review with concrete inclusion and employment-program details.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 target-period disability-inclusion evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to the Modi government’s disability policy, though effects depend on state and private-sector follow-through.","contrary_evidence":["Event counts and MoUs do not by themselves prove broad access or equal opportunity.","The ministry framing is self-reported and likely optimistic."],"lenses":["6B.3","6B.4","6B.8","6B.9","6B.10"]}

**Search and Rejection Summary**
I considered roughly 30 documents across WHO, World Bank, PIB year-end reviews, Ministry releases, and supplementary program notes. I retained 16 total URLs for the dossier: 6 reused items and 10 new source-claim units.

I rejected or collapsed:
- Later 2023-2026 updates unless they directly back-reported 2022 implementation.
- Duplicate PIB releases that repeated the same Jal Jeevan, PMGKAY, or education figures without adding a distinct claim.
- Generic program explainers that lacked dated 2022 delivery detail.
- State-specific or sector-specific notes that were too detached from the central ruler’s attributable choices.
- Purely promotional material that restated existing claims without new implementation or outcome information.

**Remaining Gaps**
- Independent household-survey evidence for 2022 nutrition, learning, disability access, and subnational equity is still thin.
- Most strong 2022 evidence is official self-reporting; external audits or evaluations would improve confidence.
- The record is stronger on inputs and service counts than on final outcomes like learning gains, nutrition status, or sustained poverty reduction.
- More search would help on cross-checked outcomes and on whether benefits were evenly distributed across regions and social groups.
